Require an `Idempotency-Key` header from the client, store it on the row, and enforce uniqueness in the DB rather than with a pre-check (a plain "look then insert" has a race between two retries).

Add a migration: `idempotency_key text`, `UNIQUE (account_id, idempotency_key)` on `orders`. Then in `routes/orders.js`:

```js
router.post("/", async (req, res) => {
  const key = req.get("Idempotency-Key");
  if (!key) return res.status(400).json({ error: "Idempotency-Key header required" });

  try {
    const row = await db.write(
      "INSERT INTO orders (account_id, sku, quantity, idempotency_key) VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4) RETURNING *",
      [req.user.accountId, req.body.sku, req.body.quantity, key]
    );
    res.status(201).json(row);
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.code === "23505") { // unique_violation
      const [existing] = await db.query(
        "SELECT * FROM orders WHERE account_id = $1 AND idempotency_key = $2",
        [req.user.accountId, key]
      );
      return res.status(200).json(existing);
    }
    throw err;
  }
});
```

This fits the existing shape: `db.write` already wraps the insert in `BEGIN`/`COMMIT`, so the unique constraint is checked atomically — no extra locking needed. Scope the key to `account_id` so two different accounts can reuse the same client-generated key.

One gap worth flagging: this treats a replayed key as "same order," not "same payload." If a client reuses a key with a different `sku`/`quantity`, it'll silently get back the original order. If that matters, compare the stored payload (or a hash of it) to the request on conflict and return 409 on mismatch instead of 200.

