Both `sessionUser` runs first and puts `req.user.accountId` on every request, so the fix is to add `AND account_id = $n` to each `UPDATE` and pass `req.user.accountId`, checking that a row actually came back (IDOR otherwise: any logged-in user can patch/cancel/delete another account's row by guessing its id).

`routes/accounts.js`:
```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```
(here `id` *is* the account id, so the scoping clause is `WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4` — really just `WHERE id = $3` since `$3` should be `req.user.accountId` itself, not `req.params.id`; a user should only ever be able to patch/delete their own account, so drop `:id` entirely and use `req.user.accountId` as the sole predicate).

Same pattern for delete:
```js
router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 RETURNING id",
    [req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.status(204).end();
});
```

`routes/orders.js` — orders belong to an account but aren't keyed by it, so keep `:id` and add the scoping predicate:
```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

`db.write` currently returns `res.rows[0]`, which is `undefined` (falsy) when the scoped `WHERE` matches nothing — that's what the `if (!row)` checks rely on, so no change needed there.

Add one logging middleware in `app.js`, mounted after `sessionUser` so it can attribute the request to an account, listening on the response's `finish` event so it captures status and duration regardless of how the route handler resolves:

```js
app.use(sessionUser);
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = Date.now();
  res.on("finish", () => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      method: req.method,
      path: req.path,
      status: res.statusCode,
      ms: Date.now() - start,
      accountId: req.user && req.user.accountId,
    }));
  });
  next();
});
```

This covers every route under `/accounts` and `/orders` without touching either route file, and `finish` fires even for the hung-request case discussed earlier only if a response is eventually sent — a request that never gets a `res.json`/`res.status` call still won't log, which is consistent with there being nothing to report yet.
